SUMMARY:Lines of Resistance: Graphic Storytelling\, Minidoka\, and the Memory of Incarceration
DESCRIPTION:Please note: this event is off site at 220 NW 2nd Ave. \n\n\n\nLines of ResistanceSaturday May 16\, 1PM220 NW 2nd AvenuePortland\, Oregon 97209 \n\n\n\nRSVP\n\n\n\nThe Japanese American Museum of Oregon invites you to a special conversation\, inspired by the current exhibition\, Minidoka on Our Minds. \n\n\n\nKelly Goto (Seattle Samurai) and Frank Abe (We Hereby Refuse) will explore how graphic storytelling gives voice to the lived experiences of Japanese Americans incarcerated at the Minidoka concentration camp during World War II. Award-winning journalist Lori Matsukawa will moderate. \n\n\n\nBetween 1942 and 1945\, more than 13\,000 Japanese Americans — many from Oregon and Washington — were confined at Minidoka. While official records document policies and numbers\, art and memoir illuminate the human dimension: the families who endured\, the communities that rebuilt\, and the individuals who resisted. \n\n\n\nThrough cartoons and graphic narrative\, Seattle Samurai reflects on intergenerational memory and cultural resilience in the Pacific Northwest. We Hereby Refuse foregrounds acts of principled dissent — draft resisters and those who challenged loyalty oaths — expanding our understanding of resistance within the camps. \n\n\n\nSet against JAMO’s Minidoka on Our Minds exhibit\, this conversation considers how drawn lines—ink on paper—become acts of remembrance and conscience. How does art help us see Minidoka not only as history\, but as lived experience? What lessons about civil liberties and belonging continue to resonate today? \n\n\n\nJoin us for a discussion that connects place\, memory\, and storytelling—and explores how graphic memoir transforms incarceration history into shared civic reflection. \n\n\n\nBook signing to follow – books by each author\, published through Chin Music Press\, will be available for purchase at the event. \n\n\n\nKelly Goto \n\n\n\nKelly Goto is the author of Seattle Samurai: A Cartoonist’s Perspective of the Japanese American Experience\, a visual memoir honoring the life and work of her father\, cartoonist Sam Goto. A fourth-generation Japanese American with deep roots in the Pacific Northwest\, she explores themes of incarceration\, intergenerational memory\, identity\, and cultural resilience through graphic storytelling. In addition to her work as an author\, Goto is an internationally recognized design strategist and founder of gotomedia\, bringing a lifelong commitment to visual communication and narrative systems to her work preserving and interpreting Japanese American history. \n\n\n\nFrank Abe \n\n\n\nFRANK ABE is lead author of the graphic novel\, WE HEREBY REFUSE: Japanese American Resistance to Wartime Incarceration\, a Finalist in Creative Nonfiction for the Washington State Book Award\, and co-editor with Floyd Cheung of the Penguin Classics anthology\, THE LITERATURE OF JAPANESE AMERICAN INCARCERATION. He won an American Book Award as co-editor of JOHN OKADA: The Life & Rediscovered Work of the Author of No-No Boy and wrote and directed the award-winning PBS documentary CONSCIENCE AND THE CONSTITUTION. He is featured in the podcast Rachel Maddow Presents BURN ORDER and is currently developing a new stage adaptation of Okada’s NO-NO BOY. He blogs at Resisters.com. \n\n\n\nLori Matsukawa \n\n\n\nLori Matsukawa is an Emmy Award–winning broadcast journalist and former news anchor with more than 40 years in the industry. A respected community leader and storyteller\, she has long been engaged in issues of civil rights\, public memory\, and Japanese American history. Matsukawa has served on numerous nonprofit and cultural boards and remains an active voice in community dialogue. Her first children’s book\, Brave Mrs. Sato based on her real-life babysitter and real-life grandmother who was a picture bride\, was published in November 2023. Her memoir Being There: Memoir of an Asian American Journalist is scheduled for publication in April 2026. She brings deep experience\, insight\, and regional perspective to her role as moderator for this conversation. \n\n\n\n\n\n\n                \n                        \n                            RSVP for Lines of Resistance\n                             \n                        \n                        Name(Required)\n                            \n                            \n                                                    \n                                                    First\n                                                \n                            \n                            \n                                                    \n                                                    Last\n                                                \n                            \n                        Email(Required)\n                            \n                        Including yourself\, how many people are you registering for?(Required)Zip CodeWould you like to be kept up-to-date with our upcoming programs and events? Yes\, please!
URL:https://jamo.org/event/lines-of-resistance/
LOCATION:220 PDX\, 220 NW 2nd Ave\, Portland\, Oregon\, 97209\, United States
CATEGORIES:Artist Talk,Books and Literature
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://jamo.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/04/Lines-of-Resistance-Flyer.jpg
END:VEVENT
